# EAS Deploy Only

All builds for distribution go through **EAS Build** (Expo Application Services). All store submissions go through **EAS Submit**. Local Xcode archive + manual Transporter / Play Console upload is forbidden in production paths.

## Why

- Reproducible builds (consistent Node, bun, Expo SDK, Xcode versions)
- Source-mapped Sentry uploads happen as part of the build pipeline
- Provisioning + signing managed centrally (no "works on my machine")
- Audit trail per build in EAS dashboard
- Versioning + build numbers auto-incremented consistently

## Build profiles (`eas.json`)

| Profile | Distribution | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `development` | Internal | Day-to-day dev on physical devices with Expo Dev Client |
| `preview` | Internal (TestFlight internal, Play internal track) | Internal beta + ticket QA |
| `staging` | Internal (TestFlight internal) | Pointed at staging backend for E2E checks |
| `production` | Store | App Store + Play Store production submissions |

## Commands

```bash
# Day-to-day dev build (one-time per device)
eas build --profile development --platform ios
eas build --profile development --platform android

# Internal beta after merging a feature
eas build --profile preview --platform all

# Production build (only after staging is verified + JP confirms)
eas build --profile production --platform all

# Submit to stores
eas submit --profile production --platform ios
eas submit --profile production --platform android

# Hotfix without store re-review (JS-only changes)
eas update --branch production --message "MOBILE-XXX: hotfix description"
```

## Pull-before-build rule

Before EVERY production build:

```bash
git pull --rebase origin main
bun install
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun test
./scripts/sync-from-web.sh --check  # ensure no drift
```

If anything fails, fix it before building. Never push a build with red tests.

## Versioning

- `app.json`: `version` is the human version shown in stores (e.g. `1.4.2`)
- iOS `buildNumber` + Android `versionCode` auto-incremented by EAS via `cli.appVersionSource: remote`
- Tag every production build in git: `git tag mobile-v1.4.2 && git push --tags`

## Forbidden practices

- Manual Xcode `Archive` for App Store submission (use `eas submit`)
- Manual `./gradlew bundleRelease` for Play upload (use `eas submit`)
- Bumping `version` without a corresponding git tag
- Shipping `eas update` to production without first verifying on `staging` branch
- Hardcoded env values in `app.json` — always via EAS Secrets

## EAS Update (OTA) rules

EAS Update can ship JS-only fixes without a store review. **It cannot ship native code changes** (new native modules, native config changes).

Use cases for OTA:
- Bug fix in a screen / hook / business logic
- Copy / wording change
- Small UI tweak

DO NOT use OTA for:
- Anything touching `app.json` `plugins` or native config
- Adding a new native module (`expo-*` with native side)
- Changing SDK version
- Security-critical fixes (push the native build instead so OTA isn't bypassed by users on old binaries)

Always tag OTA updates: `eas update --branch production --message "MOBILE-XXX-NNN: ..."` so the audit trail shows what shipped.

## Staged rollout

iOS App Store has phased release (Apple-managed 7-day rollout).
Play Store: use staged rollout per release (10% → 25% → 50% → 100%) — pause if Sentry crash rate spikes.

## Rollback

- **OTA:** `eas update --branch production --republish [previous-update-id]`
- **Native iOS:** "Expedited review" request + ship previous version. Apple usually approves within hours for critical bugs.
- **Native Android:** Pause rollout in Play Console; promote previous version from internal track.

Full release runbook lives in `MOBILE-LAUNCH-007` Trello ticket.
